---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/coderturtle/wade-in";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const recentEntries = (await getCollection("buildlog", ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Wade In: guide"
  description="Open a real terminal for the first time, and leave able to get real work done with Claude Code."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Wade In</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-lg opacity-80">
    Open a real terminal for the first time, and leave able to get real work done with Claude Code.
  </p>

  <div class="post-body mt-10">
    <p>
      No coding background. No command-line experience. This workshop starts from a learner who has
      never opened a terminal before and walks through ten modules, inside one continuing story, to
      someone who can open a terminal, launch Claude Code, and do real research, document, and
      spreadsheet-adjacent work with it, safely, on their own.
    </p>

    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
    <p>
      Non-technical knowledge workers who have never used a terminal or a command line. A computer
      you control (can install software on), and a paid Claude account, are the prerequisites named
      so far. Exact platform support (macOS, Windows, Linux) and the exact account/installation
      details are still open design questions, not finalized yet - see the design doc if you want
      the specifics as they firm up.
    </p>

    <h2>Two checks, every module (once each module's real exercise exists)</h2>
    <p>
      First, a <strong>required checklist</strong>: a short script, planned to ship inside the
      workshop folder, that will check your work against a set of real facts, not a subjective "is
      this good" call. Second, a <strong>short write-up, graded for you</strong>: a quick answer in
      your own words, checked by an AI pass, for the judgment calls a script can't make on its own.
      Neither exists yet for any module - see Current status, below.
    </p>

    <h2>The runbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Prerequisites: a computer you control, and a paid Claude account with Claude Code access.</li>
      <li>
        Start at <a href={`${base}modules/`}>the modules index</a>. It has the full arc, in order,
        with what each one is about - the module pages themselves are still being built (see
        Current status, below).
      </li>
      <li>
        Work through the ten modules in order. Module 01 is a hard prerequisite for everything
        after it - nothing past it assumes you already know how to open a terminal.
      </li>
      <li>
        No module completes by reading it. Every module states a required gate, checked two ways.
        If a module ever reduces to "read this, then move on," or a link here is broken, that's a
        defect: <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <p>
      Module 01 has real, authored content and a working checker script - try it if you're set up
      for it. Modules 02-10 are still skeleton only: a decided question, arc position, gate design,
      and takeaway shape, but no authored exercise yet. That's real work still ahead, not a
      formality. Watch the build log below for progress.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    recentEntries.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 class="mt-14 text-xl font-semibold">Recent from the build log</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {recentEntries.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`${base}build-log/${entry.id}/`} class="entry-card">
                <p class="entry-title text-lg font-semibold">{entry.data.title}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm opacity-80">{entry.data.description}</p>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm opacity-70">
          See the <a href={`${base}build-log/`} class="underline">full index</a>.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
